<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"><title>{title} | PointCast</title><meta name="description" content={description}></head><body><header><a href="/">POINTCAST ↗</a><span>THE DATA FIELD GUIDE / 2026</span></header><main><section class="hero"><span class="badge">FIELD NOTE 01 · THE GROUND</span><h1>A small circle,<br>28 earthquakes.</h1><p class="intro">A five-year catalog of magnitude 3 and larger earthquakes inside a 25-mile circle near El Segundo. Each dot is an event, not a prediction.</p></section><figure class="art" style="margin:0"><img src="/data-stories/earthquake-illustration.png" alt="" width="1536" height="1024" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;border:2px solid #192638;border-radius:20px"><figcaption>Conceptual illustration · not a map or data graphic</figcaption></figure><section class="panel dark"><span class="tag">THE STUDY AREA</span><h2>One point. A 25-mile radius. Five years.</h2><p>The circle is centered at approximately 33.9192° N, 118.4165° W. It is a geographic query, not a city boundary. Events can be offshore or in other communities.</p><p>USGS ComCat records from January 2020 through December 2024, retrieved November 3, 2026. All 28 returned records were reviewed. The catalog can be revised.</p></section><section class="panel"><span class="tag">THE CATALOG / UTC YEAR</span><h2>Every dot has a date.</h2><p>Counts within this specific filter. Clusters and aftershocks are included; dots are not independent episodes.</p><div class="legend"><span><i class="dot"></i> Magnitude 3 to under 4</span><span><i class="dot" style="background:#f5bd43"></i> Magnitude 4 or greater</span></div><div class="year"><strong>2020</strong><div class="dots" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball big"></span></div><strong>2</strong></div><div class="year"><strong>2021</strong><div class="dots" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball big"></span><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball big"></span><span class="ball "></span></div><strong>7</strong></div><div class="year"><strong>2022</strong><div class="dots" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ball "></span></div><strong>1</strong></div><div class="year"><strong>2023</strong><div class="dots" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ball big"></span><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball "></span></div><strong>3</strong></div><div class="year"><strong>2024</strong><div class="dots" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ball big"></span><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball big"></span><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball big"></span><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball "></span><span class="ball "></span></div><strong>15</strong></div><p>The 2024 cluster is not evidence of a long-term trend. Detection and catalog completeness can vary, and this short window cannot estimate future hazard or shaking at an address.</p></section><section class="panel"><h2>Open the field notes.</h2><p>Origin dates are UTC. Depth is in kilometers. Place names and magnitudes come from USGS; one magnitude is rounded to two decimal places for display.</p><details><summary>View all 28 records</summary><table><thead><tr><th>Date (UTC)</th><th>USGS place</th><th>Magnitude</th><th>Depth (km)</th><th>Event</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2020-04-22</td><td>1km S of View Park-Windsor Hills, CA</td><td>3.69</td><td>11.6</td><td><a href="https://earthquake.usgs.gov/earthquakes/eventpage/ci39400304">ci39400304</a></td></tr><tr><td>2020-09-19</td><td>3km WSW of South El Monte, CA</td><td>4.54</td><td>16.87</td><td><a href="https://earthquake.usgs.gov/earthquakes/eventpage/ci38695658">ci38695658</a></td></tr><tr><td>2021-01-20</td><